   "html_text": "However, others thought differently. During the ‘Priestley Riots’ in July 1791, a ‘Church and King’ mob burned Priestley’s home and laboratory to the ground. To the mob’s organizers and sympathizers, any work “Gunpowder Joe” Priestley praised so highly would be anathema. Thus, while the Observations kept its status as a “holy book” for those committed to religious, social and political reform, these were an embattled minority in England, especially during and after the French Revolution. In addi",
   "md_text": "However, others thought differently. During the ‘Priestley Riots’ in July 1791, a ‘Church and King’ mob burned Priestley’s home and laboratory to the ground. To the mob’s organizers and sympathizers, any work “Gunpowder Joe” Priestley praised so highly would be anathema. Thus, while the Observations kept its status as a “holy book” for those committed to religious, social and political reform, these were an embattled minority in England, especially during and after the French Revolution. In addi"
